1
00:00:00,210 --> 00:00:09,090
Today, I'm interviewing Reiner Pope, who is the CEO of MatX, which is a new chip startup. Previously, he was doing TPU architecture and many other things at Google.

2
00:00:09,090 --> 00:00:16,480
This is a very different format from my usual interviews. This is going to be a blackboard lecture. We're going to get up in a second. We in fact built this whole new studio with specifically this

3
00:00:16,480 --> 00:00:25,370
format in mind, so it's a pleasure to get to inaugurate it with you. We're going to be talking about model architecture, ML infra, and many other things.

4
00:00:25,370 --> 00:00:35,696
The reason I think it's an important topic is because once you understand how training and inference work in a cluster, a lot of things—about why AI is the way

5
00:00:35,696 --> 00:00:45,550
it is, why AI architectures are the way they are, why API prices are the way they are, and fundamentally why AI progress is the way it is—start making sense.

6
00:00:45,550 --> 00:00:52,859
You need to understand the details to get there, and you need a blackboard to understand the details. Reiner, thank you so much for doing this. Very happy to be here.

7
00:00:52,859 --> 00:01:05,150
Full disclosure, I am an angel investor in MatX, but that's unrelated to this podcast. Reiner, to kick us off I'll ask this question. We have a couple of companies like Claude and Codex and Cursor

8
00:01:05,150 --> 00:01:14,640
offering something like Fast Mode, where for 6x the price, they'll stream you tokens at 2.5x the speed. Mechanically, I'm curious what's going on here.

9
00:01:14,640 --> 00:01:27,220
Why is it the case that you can pay more to get faster latency? Two, could you keep going? Could you pay 100x more and somehow get much faster speeds? Three, could you go the other way?

10
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:36,820
Could you have something like Claude Code "Slow Mode", where if you are willing to wait for minutes on end, you could get even cheaper prices?

11
00:01:36,820 --> 00:01:44,049
Maybe this will help motivate the analysis that you'll be doing through the lecture. Great. To jump to the conclusion a little bit, the big effect is batch size.

12
00:01:44,049 --> 00:01:53,780
What we're going to do now is quantify exactly what that looks like and what its implications are on latency and cost. There's another effect, which you can call speculative decoding

13
00:01:53,780 --> 00:02:04,170
or multi-token prediction. We can maybe come back to that later, but the first thing that we'll talk through is batch size. What I'd like to introduce is the two principles of analysis.

14
00:02:04,170 --> 00:02:19,190
First, we're going to look at a roofline analysis of how we run a transformer model on a cluster of chips. We'll take a Blackwell NVL72 cluster, so a rack of 72 GPUs.

15
00:02:19,190 --> 00:02:27,890
The roofline analysis means we look at memory bandwidth and compute performance. The other side of that is that we're going to look at just two simple

16
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:42,289
factors of the model: the time to operate on the weights, and the time to operate on the context, the KV cache. Let's jump in. We're going to try and estimate the time that it takes to run

17
00:02:42,289 --> 00:02:52,490
an inference of a certain shape. We're not perfect here. We can't exactly predict the time, so instead we're going to approximate. We're going to say that the time must be greater than or

18
00:02:52,490 --> 00:03:07,310
equal to a certain quantity. We're going to consider two different aspects: the time it takes to do the memory fetches, and the time it takes to do the compute.

19
00:03:07,310 --> 00:03:19,750
It will turn out that this gives us very strong predictive power, even with a simple model. One by one, what is the time that it takes to do the compute?

20
00:03:19,750 --> 00:03:28,110
There are really two things I need to do in the compute. I need to multiply by all of the active parameters, and then I need to do some work on the attention.

21
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:38,340
Multiplying by all the active parameters, I have a certain batch size that I'm running, and I've got a number of active parameters in my model.

22
00:03:38,340 --> 00:03:48,350
Then I'm just going to divide this by the compute throughput, which is the FLOPs of the chip. This is a hardware concern.

23
00:03:48,350 --> 00:04:00,770
This accounts for all of the compute time for all of the weight matrix multiplies. There's a little caveat here. We've ignored the time to do any of the attention computation, but that in general

24
00:04:00,770 --> 00:04:09,009
will be quite small in comparison to this. So we'll ignore this. I'll just interrupt from time to time to ask some very naive questions or to clarify some basic points.

25
00:04:09,009 --> 00:04:17,540
For the audience, you're not serving one user at a time. The batch refers to the fact that you're serving many different users at the same time, and that's a whole batch.

26
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:28,070
I can motivate the batch at least a little bit. We will see exactly why batch is such a favorable optimization. What will turn out to be the case is that if you do not batch together many

27
00:04:28,070 --> 00:04:40,920
users, the cost and the economics you get can be a thousand times worse than if you do batch many users together. We'll be able to see that quite explicitly. Then, number of active parameters.

28
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:52,210
If I look at, for example, a DeepSeek model, the DeepSeek V3 model has about 37 billion active parameters, and 700 billion total parameters.

29
00:04:52,210 --> 00:05:00,910
We're focusing on just the ones that are active for a single AI token. We're modeling compute performance. I'm going to keep writing equals, but in all of these cases, you can think of this

30
00:05:00,910 --> 00:05:09,630
time as being at least this much, and maybe there will be some terms we ignored. On the memory side, what do we need to do with memory?

31
00:05:09,630 --> 00:05:21,589
We need to fetch all of the weights, so there is some time to fetch the total number of parameters, not just the active parameters.

32
00:05:21,589 --> 00:05:36,870
There's weight fetch time, and then in addition, there's a KV cache fetch time. This actually depends on batch size. For every element of the batch, we have to fetch an entire context

33
00:05:36,870 --> 00:05:52,770
length worth of tokens, and there's a size per token, bytes for one token. This is a model parameter. Maybe just backing up, let's explain what the KV cache is real quick.

34
00:05:52,770 --> 00:06:06,640
When I do a forward pass… Let me draw how the autoregressive inference works. This is during decode. If I have a bunch of text tokens… I'm drawing a tensor because ultimately

35
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:22,870
the tokens are represented as a tensor in some embedding dimension. In this direction, I have the sequence length. The work of running a decode is that I have to run each token through

36
00:06:22,870 --> 00:06:35,770
a whole bunch of matrix multiplies over a bunch of different layers. In general, I'm going to have to do that work over all of these tokens.

37
00:06:35,770 --> 00:06:50,360
But one step of decode is to produce just this one additional token up here. What I'm going to do there is run a full forward pass of multiplying by all of the weight matrices in the entire model.

38
00:06:50,360 --> 00:07:03,690
But then I've got this attention mechanism where this token is looking at all of the past tokens, and what is it looking at specifically? It is looking at some internal representation that the model

39
00:07:03,690 --> 00:07:13,440
has produced of the tokens, and we call that the KV cache. This process of this single token attending to all of the history of tokens is attention.

40
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:23,309
It is mostly dominated by memory fetches rather than matrix multiplies. So we've got the amount of memory that we're fetching shown over here,

41
00:07:23,309 --> 00:07:41,250
and then this is of course just divided by the memory bandwidth, so the memory bytes per second. In fact, these equations here are enough for us to now draw some fit lines.

42
00:07:41,250 --> 00:07:55,300
The things that we'd like to look at are sensitivity to batch, and then also, which we'll draw separately, to context length. We said that the big effect you can get is some trade-off in

43
00:07:55,300 --> 00:08:11,710
latency versus cost in batch size. Let's draw them out. I think there are just really two graphs that we want to draw. We'll first draw batch size versus time here.

44
00:08:11,710 --> 00:08:27,890
When we look at the shape of this, we've got a maximum of the sum and then another term. Let's look at these terms one by one and how they scale: the time for compute and memory, and how they show up.

45
00:08:27,890 --> 00:08:44,260
Let's first look at this compute time. This is just purely linear in batch size with no offset, so it is some curve like this. This is t compute.

46
00:08:44,260 --> 00:09:14,349
On the memory side, we've got some portion here that is just this constant in some base offset here, which is the weight fetch. Finally, we have this term here, which is the KV fetch, which is pretty

47
00:09:14,349 --> 00:09:29,099
linear in batch size, and so it looks like that. The sum of this plus this maxed with this… Let's at least first draw the sum.

48
00:09:29,099 --> 00:09:47,399
The two memory times in conjunction end up looking on this curved slope like this. Then the overall maximum is—I'll draw a little thicker here—the maximum of these two curves. What does this mean?

49
00:09:47,399 --> 00:10:11,150
This is a latency plot. If I grow my batch size, initially I get some not very strong dependence on batch size, so there is some lower bound on latency here.

50
00:10:11,150 --> 00:10:20,580
This already partially answers the question. For a given hardware configuration—and we can talk about varying the hardware configuration—there is a lower bound on latency.

51
00:10:20,580 --> 00:10:34,380
It is simply that I need to read all of my total parameters from memory into the chips, and that takes a certain amount of time. If I use all of my memory bandwidth, I can't do any better than that.

52
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:49,580
It seems like the way you've drawn the slopes for compute time and how the KV grows—and what implication the KV has on memory time— What if this were above or below?

53
00:10:49,580 --> 00:11:00,969
Yeah, is that necessarily the case? If this is always true, then as batch size grows compute always dominates KV, which suggests that if you have a big enough batch size,

54
00:11:00,969 --> 00:11:08,270
maybe memory is never an issue. This is really sensitive to the context length, so I think we should come back and explore this.

55
00:11:08,270 --> 00:11:15,490
As you vary the context length, the KV fetch time will go up and up, and that will cause a transition from compute-limited to memory-limited.

56
00:11:15,490 --> 00:11:29,790
Is there something especially significant about the slope being exactly the slope of the compute time? Whenever we have balance points, it says that you're getting it exactly right.

57
00:11:29,790 --> 00:11:40,660
For the particular context length where the slopes match, that says I am equally memory-bound and compute-bound, which is a really desirable place to be.

58
00:11:40,660 --> 00:11:54,650
This is a very simple algebra problem, but suppose the optimal is 100K context length, and you go to 200K context length. Does your MFU go down to 50%?

59
00:11:54,650 --> 00:12:08,620
Does it have a humongous impact on MFU to be slightly outside of the optimal context length range, the Goldilocks zone? That's right. That is true as modeled here. There is a key point here that

60
00:12:08,620 --> 00:12:20,000
I'm modeling the memory fetch as linear in context length. That depends on model architecture. It is true for all of the model architectures with dense attention.

61
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:27,900
Sparse attention actually scales much better than that. Got it. Is sparse attention what everybody uses in practice? I'm pretty excited about sparse attention.

62
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:36,199
It's hard to know what the labs are using. DeepSeek has published a sparse attention mechanism. I'll just put a plug in that some of the DeepSeek papers that have

63
00:12:36,199 --> 00:12:49,660
published sparse attention end up putting a square root in this term. So far, we've looked at the latency. It's hard to read off cost from this. If I think about what cost means…

64
00:12:49,660 --> 00:12:59,760
To run this inference, I'm going to use the GPU for a certain number of seconds, like one millisecond or 20 milliseconds. I have to pay the rental time for that time.

65
00:12:59,760 --> 00:13:12,509
So it's $2/hour per GPU or something like that. That's the cost of this inference, but how many tokens have I processed during that inference? That is the batch size.

66
00:13:12,509 --> 00:13:34,480
What we actually want to plot is the cost versus batch size, which is t over B versus batch size. This is the cost per token. We have to imagine dividing each of these three curves by B, so

67
00:13:34,480 --> 00:13:52,520
multiplying by this reciprocal. What we end up with there is… The compute curve was linear. We divide by B, and that makes it a constant here. This is t compute.

68
00:13:52,520 --> 00:14:22,880
The KV fetch was linear, and now it becomes a constant as well. Then the weight fetch was constant, and now we've divided by B, so it becomes this parabola.

69
00:14:22,880 --> 00:14:41,289
Again, we're going to compute the max of the sum. The sum of these two terms shifts the parabola up. The sum of the KV fetch and the weight fetch gives us a higher parabola that's like this.

70
00:14:41,289 --> 00:14:54,870
Then we're going to take the max with the compute here. We end up with this being the overall shape that we care about. Again, we see some limiting behavior.

71
00:14:54,870 --> 00:15:07,020
The cost initially starts very high at a batch size of one. It almost goes to infinity because we've got so many weight fetches that are not amortized over a large batch size.

72
00:15:07,020 --> 00:15:18,639
But as we increase the batch size, the weight fetches become amortized over so many different batch elements that their cost grows very small, and eventually the compute time ends up driving the cost.

73
00:15:18,639 --> 00:15:35,910
So there is a limiting lower bound on cost, which is this line here. So Claude Code Slow or Codex Slow or whatever would just live on this line.

74
00:15:35,910 --> 00:15:46,790
It wouldn't help much because you're not able to amortize the KV values over a much bigger batch. They're unique per batch. The compute is also unique per batch.

75
00:15:46,790 --> 00:16:04,880
So what is the minimum work you can do per batch after amortizing everything else away? This point where you are no longer memory bandwidth bound, practically how big a batch do you need?

76
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:15,580
How big are the batches practically for frontier models? You can just solve for that. It's not even particularly sensitive to model architecture. Let's go ahead and do that.

77
00:16:15,580 --> 00:16:30,119
What we're talking about is when the memory time is equal to the compute time. That’s what that question is. Because we're focused on what the batch size is—and really there's a question

78
00:16:30,119 --> 00:16:38,290
of when the weights are amortized over the multiplies—I'm going to focus on comparing the weight fetch time to the weight multiply time.

79
00:16:38,290 --> 00:16:58,030
I'm going to disregard the KV fetch term just to simplify the analysis so we can get a clean answer out. We're going to equate this portion with these two times.

80
00:16:58,030 --> 00:17:22,559
Writing that out, we get N, number of total parameters, over memory bandwidth, is equal to batch size times number of active parameters divided by the compute performance.

81
00:17:22,559 --> 00:17:31,250
Looking over here, everything on the top are model parameters. Everything on the bottom are hardware parameters. It turns out to be nice to rearrange them such that we have

82
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:56,300
the hardware parameters on one side. This is equivalent to FLOPs over memory bandwidth being equal to batch size times number of active parameters, divided by the number of total parameters.

83
00:17:56,300 --> 00:18:07,900
This hardware parameter ends up being a dimensionless constant. If you look in terms of FLOPs… What are the dimensions of this? This is multiplies per second. This is bytes per second.

84
00:18:07,900 --> 00:18:25,430
So that's not quite dimensionless. But what you do is you say, how many FP4 multiplies per second times the fact that each FP4 is half a byte. I can actually make this end up being dimensionless.

85
00:18:25,430 --> 00:18:41,850
On most GPUs, this ends up being somewhere around 300. Has that ratio changed over time as we've gone from model generation to model generation, where the FLOPs keep increasing?

86
00:18:41,850 --> 00:18:53,900
This is a hardware parameter. To what extent has the hardware changed? From A100 to H100 to B100, the FLOPs have increased substantially, the memory bandwidth has also increased substantially, and it

87
00:18:53,900 --> 00:19:05,849
has remained reasonably stable. We can express this one as well. This is a sparsity parameter. I might even phrase this slightly differently. Let's solve for batch size in total.

88
00:19:05,849 --> 00:19:21,719
Moving this back over to the other side, we end up with batch size needs to be bigger than approximately 300 times sparsity. For example, in DeepSeek I activate 32

89
00:19:21,719 --> 00:19:33,130
out of 256 experts, so this would be 8 for DeepSeek. This actually gives you a ballpark which is remarkably accurate to practice. Generally, people will go a little bit larger than this.

90
00:19:33,130 --> 00:19:44,490
They don't really want to be exactly at the balance point because real-world efficiencies aren't as good as a roofline analysis would say. But take this and maybe double or triple it.

91
00:19:44,490 --> 00:20:00,070
Okay, so it's two to three thousand tokens per batch. But then if you included the KV cache, the implication would be that the optimal batch size... Should grow larger.

92
00:20:00,070 --> 00:20:13,370
We solved for the equivalence between when compute time is equal to memory time. If I add in something that consumes more memory bandwidth, then I have less available for the weight loads.

93
00:20:13,370 --> 00:20:24,330
I need to grow the memory bandwidth more, and therefore the batch size more. This seems incredibly small. This would be less than one sequence, right?

94
00:20:24,330 --> 00:20:33,969
Keep in mind that I'm talking about the number of tokens that I'm generating one more token for. It's actually 2,000 unique sequences. Got it.

95
00:20:33,969 --> 00:20:43,080
We're just talking about a single forward pass on these sequences. You think of the batch as the number of sequences. That’s right.

96
00:20:43,080 --> 00:20:50,580
When I'm prepping for interviews, I often talk to experts in the field. So for Reiner, I chatted with two of Jane Street's engineers, Clark and Axel.

97
00:20:50,580 --> 00:20:57,589
Clark, who works on low latency trading systems, walked me through why Jane Street uses FPGAs to make sure that they have predictable nanosecond latencies.

98
00:20:57,589 --> 00:21:04,370
“You can just build these like giant grids of compute very easily that do exactly what you need that touch a hundred megabytes of SRAM and then

99
00:21:04,370 --> 00:21:13,020
get your response back in tens of nanoseconds very easily. And that's basically impossible on a CPU.” He then went on to explain why CPUs just wouldn't work for this kind of thing.

100
00:21:13,020 --> 00:21:21,100
“And so if you have a clock that's going every three nanoseconds, you actually have several bytes of information at a time to make your decision.

101
00:21:21,100 --> 00:21:27,730
That's as opposed to a CPU where you'll just collect up a whole packet, you know, let's say a 1500-byte packet, and then you say, okay, this packet's ready. Here you go, CPU.

102
00:21:27,730 --> 00:21:34,879
You can start thinking about it now.” FPGAs allow you to react to the earliest part of the packet as it arrives, rather than having to wait for the full thing.

103
00:21:34,879 --> 00:21:46,059
We also talked about liquid cooling, network design, and many other things. If you're interested in this stuff, Jane Street is hiring. You can check out their open roles at JaneStreet.com/Dwarkesh.

104
00:21:46,059 --> 00:21:56,010
And if you want to watch the full prep conversation, we posted it there too. If you've got a frontier model and you are actually doing inference, surely they must

105
00:21:56,010 --> 00:22:08,169
have more than 2,000 concurrent users. Is there any added latency from the fact that you need to have the whole batch fill up? Or if you have a reasonable amount of users, is it so unlikely that

106
00:22:08,169 --> 00:22:25,460
it would take you 100 milliseconds to fill up the next 2,000 slots? The way to think about this is: when does the train depart, as a model? Let's say I've picked a batch size that I'm going to run at.

107
00:22:25,460 --> 00:22:32,860
By the way, this intersection point is the same intersection point here. I pick this batch size, and I know that it's going to take, for

108
00:22:32,860 --> 00:22:56,420
example, 20 milliseconds, which is a common place this ends up landing. This is a timeline of what is running on the GPU. It's going to start a new batch every 20 milliseconds regardless.

109
00:22:56,420 --> 00:23:05,109
You can think of this as a schedule for the train. A new train departs every 20 milliseconds. Any passengers who are ready board the train. If the train is full, they wait until the next train.

110
00:23:05,109 --> 00:23:17,750
If the train is not full, the train is going to go anyway. In terms of what that means for queuing latency, the worst case is that a request arrives just after the train departed.

111
00:23:17,750 --> 00:23:27,160
It has to wait for the next train, so that's up to 20 milliseconds, and then it has to wait for that train to complete. So the worst-case latency is 40 milliseconds.

112
00:23:27,160 --> 00:23:40,159
How is the 20 milliseconds derived? It's a rule of thumb, but where it comes from is not fully explained yet. So far we've focused on memory bandwidth and compute time.

113
00:23:40,159 --> 00:23:55,670
When we look at memory, the other consideration is that we want to use all of the memory capacity we have. Generally, we're going to use all of that memory capacity to store the weights or the KVs.

114
00:23:55,670 --> 00:24:03,450
In the time of doing a forward pass, we want to read all of the memory capacity into the chip. That is capacity divided by bandwidth.

115
00:24:03,450 --> 00:24:13,090
That tends to be 20 milliseconds on many different generations of HBM. The units make sense. You would have a byte divided by bytes per second.

116
00:24:13,090 --> 00:24:36,853
For example, on the Rubin generation, it is something like 288 gigabytes divided by 20 terabytes per second. This comes out to about 15 milliseconds. Let me make sure I understand what this is saying.

117
00:24:36,853 --> 00:24:56,830
I understand the unit analysis. What it's saying is we can evacuate and replace the HBM in this amount of time. So we don't want to be in a situation where the HBM is not big enough that we're

118
00:24:56,830 --> 00:25:08,349
not actually able to write everything we want to it or take everything out of it. Or we don't want to be in a situation where our ability to write back and forth is so small compared...

119
00:25:08,349 --> 00:25:19,110
There are sort of two scenarios. Why don't we pick a latency that is bigger than 15 milliseconds? If I think about what that means, it means I actually have time to read the HBM twice.

120
00:25:19,110 --> 00:25:30,519
By the way, most HBM accesses are reads, not writes. It's almost all reads because the weight matrices are read-only, and almost all of the KV cache accesses are reads.

121
00:25:30,519 --> 00:25:40,840
In around 30 milliseconds, I can read all of HBM twice, but what's the point of that? I don't want to read the weight matrices twice. I don't want to read the KVs twice. Makes a ton of sense.

122
00:25:40,840 --> 00:25:52,310
A couple of quick questions. If it is the case that the optimal batch size is something like 2,000, it's totally dependent on the sparsity, not dependent on the model size or anything.

123
00:25:52,310 --> 00:26:07,020
Sparsity shows up in model size, but beyond that, it only depends on sparsity, not on scale. That's a very interesting result. One question is how much of a push towards centralization is it that

124
00:26:07,020 --> 00:26:15,520
you would have these economies of scale from inference for batching? But it seems like it's not that big a deal. Is 2,000 users at the same time a lot? It doesn't seem like a lot.

125
00:26:15,520 --> 00:26:25,049
We can do a bit of analysis on this. You can think of it in terms of number of users, but a more productive way to think of it is in terms of tokens per second.

126
00:26:25,049 --> 00:26:40,980
What does this batch size mean in terms of tokens per second of the system? Tokens per second is going to be equal to the batch size. We run a batch of tokens, and we do that

127
00:26:40,980 --> 00:26:58,150
every time interval, which is equal to the 15-millisecond or 20-millisecond number. This ends up being batch size times about 60, so 64 x B.

128
00:26:58,150 --> 00:27:20,440
This ends up being around 2,000 x 64, so 128,000 tokens per second. This is in more digestible units. It's hard to reason about concurrent users, but what is the global traffic for a system?

129
00:27:20,440 --> 00:27:31,230
When you look at some of the announcements, sometimes the API providers will brag about how much traffic they have. The numbers I remember from some announcements of Gemini last year

130
00:27:31,230 --> 00:27:44,929
were in the hundreds of millions of tokens per second worldwide. This is one-thousandth of that. Gemini is big. One-thousandth of Gemini is a lot.

131
00:27:44,929 --> 00:28:04,849
To actually be competitive at scale, you need to be able to serve at least one-thousandth of Gemini. That's interesting. The more sparsity you have, the less compute you need.

132
00:28:04,849 --> 00:28:14,170
It does seem that as batch sizes get bigger, compute ends up being the bottleneck, according to this analysis. Then the question is, how far can you take sparsity?

133
00:28:14,170 --> 00:28:23,160
As the sparsity ratio increases, as you have fewer active parameters relative to total parameters, how much is the performance of the model degrading?

134
00:28:23,160 --> 00:28:36,240
Is it degrading faster than you're saving compute by increasing the sparsity factor? You mean the quality of the model, rather than the speed of the model.

135
00:28:36,240 --> 00:28:50,269
Unfortunately, we're not able to answer that analytically. That is an empirical question of model quality. The best I can do is pull up a paper and answer that empirically. Should

136
00:28:50,269 --> 00:28:57,770
we pull up the paper now? This paper is "Unified Scaling Laws for Routed Language Models." It's a somewhat old paper by this stage, but one of the things they looked

